WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:07.279
62% of people claim that their pets understand
what they say.

00:00:07.279 --> 00:00:13.940
Whether or not animals can hear, recognise
and possibly even understand what humans say,

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:16.450
has always been a profound mystery.

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:21.390
Recent evidence attempts to reveal, once and
for all, whether Tony the terrier knows the

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:26.330
difference between “good boy” and “Tony,
did you chew my slippers?”

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:31.920
Dogs may respond to these sentences, but do
dogs and other animals actually understand

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:34.640
the meaning behind those sentences?

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:35.910
Or are they just well-trained?

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:46.840
You may be very surprised by what you’re
about to discover about animal perception.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:52.510
In 1984, researchers at the National Marine
Mammal Foundation in California noticed something

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:54.340
quite unusual.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:58.270
They claimed that they heard voices of people
talking around an enclosure where they kept

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:01.090
a Beluga whale named NOC.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:06.310
They were fairly certain it wasn’t anything
paranormal, after all the voices sounded so…

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:07.310
real.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:13.370
Eventually a diver was in NOC’s tank and
he noticed the strangest thing.

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:20.200
NOC, the Beluga whale, was talking to him,
in an eerily human-like voice.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.890
Incredibly, the whale reportedly told the
diver to “get out”.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:36.860
This is an actual recording of NOC imitating
human speech.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:40.439
Kinda sounds like a human talking through
a kazoo, doesn’t it?

00:01:40.439 --> 00:01:45.970
That’s because unlike humans who use their
larynx, whales use their nasal tract to produce

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:48.619
sounds, making everything sound all nasally.

00:01:48.619 --> 00:01:54.159
It’s believed that NOC, having lived most
of his life in close proximity to humans,

00:01:54.159 --> 00:01:56.110
learnt to mimic the human voice.

00:01:56.110 --> 00:02:02.810
But the real question is, was NOC merely repeating
noises he picked up from humans, or did he

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:06.380
actually understand the meaning behind what
he was saying?

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:13.040
Asian elephants, seals and parrots have also
been known to imitate human speech, but do

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:14.940
they understand what they are saying?

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:18.279
And do they understand what we say to them?

00:02:18.279 --> 00:02:24.709
In 1891 a German high school mathematics teacher
named Wilhelm Von Osten convinced himself

00:02:24.709 --> 00:02:27.849
that animals could be taught basic mathematics.

00:02:27.849 --> 00:02:32.069
He tried to teach maths to a cat, a horse
and a bear.

00:02:32.069 --> 00:02:37.159
The cat couldn’t care less and was only
interested in itself, the bear was just downright

00:02:37.159 --> 00:02:41.889
hostile towards him but the horse showed great
promise.

00:02:41.889 --> 00:02:46.950
After extensive tutelage, the horse, named
Hans, learnt to tap his hoof in response to

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:50.189
numbers Von Osten wrote on his blackboard.

00:02:50.189 --> 00:02:55.790
If Von Osten wrote the number two, Hans would
tap his hoof twice, if he wrote four, Hans

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:58.169
would tap four times, and so on.

00:02:58.169 --> 00:03:03.139
Spurred on by this success, Von Osten proceeded
to teach Hans to answer basic mathematical

00:03:03.139 --> 00:03:04.499
equations.

00:03:04.499 --> 00:03:10.989
Von Osten would write on the blackboard “2
+ 2 =” and Hans would tap his hoof four

00:03:10.989 --> 00:03:11.989
times.

00:03:11.989 --> 00:03:15.860
Von Osten was delighted and exhibited Hans
to the public all over Germany.

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:21.279
During these shows, which Von Osten never
charged admission for, the crowd were awe-stricken

00:03:21.279 --> 00:03:27.260
as Hans correctly answered an array of basic
maths equations by using his hoof to tap out

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:28.389
the answers.

00:03:28.389 --> 00:03:35.579
Hans could add, subtract, multiply, divide
and even work out the square root of a number.

00:03:35.579 --> 00:03:39.590
Hans would correctly answer around 89% of
the questions.

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:43.839
The news of “Hans the genius horse” rapidly
spread across Germany.

00:03:43.839 --> 00:03:47.739
But along with Hans’ fame came critics and
skeptics.

00:03:47.739 --> 00:03:53.139
A psychologist, Oskar Pfungst asked to do
some experiments with Hans, to which Von Osten

00:03:53.139 --> 00:03:54.439
agreed.

00:03:54.439 --> 00:03:59.779
Oskar Pfungst erected a large tent to perform
the experiments in, to eradicate the possibility

00:03:59.779 --> 00:04:03.119
of Hans being influenced by outside stimuli.

00:04:03.119 --> 00:04:08.099
As a control test, Pfungst asked Von Osten
to step inside the tent and ask Hans mathematical

00:04:08.099 --> 00:04:10.879
questions like he usually does.

00:04:10.879 --> 00:04:14.739
As expected, Hans got most of the questions
correct.

00:04:14.739 --> 00:04:20.150
However, Pfungst then asked Von Osten to move
a little farther away from Hans whilst he

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:26.610
asked the questions and subsequently, Hans
got far fewer answers correct.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:32.520
Finally, Pfungst told Von Osten to ask Hans
questions that he knew Von Osten did not know

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:33.979
the answer to.

00:04:33.979 --> 00:04:39.210
When Von Osten asked these questions, the
accuracy of Hans’ answers fell to almost

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:40.330
zero.

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:44.630
It appeared that in order for Hans to get
the answer correct, the person asking the

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:48.680
question, had to know the answer to the question
also.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:55.470
These results were very strange, but incredibly
interesting, so Pfungst investigated further.

00:04:55.470 --> 00:05:01.830
He observed Von Osten’s facial expressions
and posture whilst he was asking the questions.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:08.510
Pfungst noticed Von Osten’s facial expression
and posture change, right after he asked a

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:09.510
question.

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:13.660
His face and posture would tense up in expectation
of Hans’ answer.

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:18.160
However, each time Hans tapped his hoof and
got closer to the correct answer, Von Osten

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:19.160
would relax slightly and his posture, expressions
and mannerisms would change.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:20.160
As soon as Hans tapped his hoof enough times
so that he had reached the correct answer,

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:24.229
Von Osten’s posture and expressions would
relax and become happier, because he was relieved

00:05:24.229 --> 00:05:29.800
that Hans had seemingly arrived at the correct
answer, all by himself.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:35.490
It transpired that the horse was receiving
small visual clues that acted as feedback.

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:39.540
The horse would start tapping as soon as he
observed Von Osten asking the question and

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:41.270
then tensing up.

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:47.050
When the tension had alleviated from Von Osten’s
face, Hans would stop tapping his hoof.

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:53.210
Hans was never actually doing any mathematics;
he was simply well attuned to his owner’s

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:54.830
visual clues.

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:59.180
Von Osten was shocked at this revelation,
because he was completely unaware that he

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:02.919
was providing Hans with these unconscious
visual clues.

00:06:02.919 --> 00:06:06.150
He genuinely thought his horse was a genius.

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:11.060
The results of Pfungst’s experiment had
enormous effects on how all scientific experiments

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:13.499
would be carried out in the future.

00:06:13.499 --> 00:06:17.180
This phenomenon came to be known as “The
Clever Hans Effect”.

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:21.930
The Clever Hans Effect, as we know it today,
is when an experimenter unwittingly alters

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:28.729
the results of an experiment, simply because
he or she is expecting a certain result.

00:06:28.729 --> 00:06:35.009
The simple expectation for something to happen,
can have huge consequences on an experiment’s

00:06:35.009 --> 00:06:39.080
results, without the experimenter even realising
it.

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:44.300
These days, necessary measures are taken when
working with both animals and humans to prevent

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:48.449
the Clever Hans effect from altering the results
of experiments.

00:06:48.449 --> 00:06:54.439
A border collie named Rico came into the spotlight
in 2004 after being intensively studied by

00:06:54.439 --> 00:07:00.969
animal psychologists from the Max Planck Institute
for Evolutionary Anthropology in Germany.

00:07:00.969 --> 00:07:05.901
The researchers showed such a great interest
in Rico because his owners reported that he

00:07:05.901 --> 00:07:08.139
could understand over 200 words.

00:07:08.139 --> 00:07:10.960
A feat previously unheard of in the canine
kingdom.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:17.510
To test whether Rico’s skills were a bunch
of fluff or a truly bone-a-fide talent, the

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:19.939
researchers set up an experiment.

00:07:19.939 --> 00:07:24.909
The researchers arranged 200 toys on the floor
in a room adjacent to were Rico was being

00:07:24.909 --> 00:07:28.400
held, they did this ten toys at a time.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:33.150
Each toy had a unique name, such as “fluffy”
or “squeezy”; Rico’s owner had already

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:36.020
trained him to remember the name of each toy.

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:41.240
Each time the researchers let Rico into the
room with the toys and asked Rico to fetch

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:44.440
a toy, then another toy and another.

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:47.500
Until Rico had fetched all 10 toys.

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:52.349
Whilst the researchers were issuing commands
to Rico, they stayed on the other side of

00:07:52.349 --> 00:07:57.800
a dividing wall, where Rico could hear them
but not see them, to eliminate the Clever

00:07:57.800 --> 00:07:58.800
Hans effect.

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:05.039
In total, Rico successfully remembered and
retrieved 93% of the toys.

00:08:05.039 --> 00:08:11.110
Impressive, but this was only a test of Rico’s
memory, not his cognitive function – ie.

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:15.279
his ability to use logic and inference, just
like a human.

00:08:15.279 --> 00:08:17.979
So the researchers did a second experiment.

00:08:17.979 --> 00:08:23.340
They arranged seven items in the room with
an eight item which was brand new, which they

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:25.499
gave a unique name to.

00:08:25.499 --> 00:08:29.849
Rico had never seen or heard the name of this
new item before.

00:08:29.849 --> 00:08:34.099
Amazingly when Rico was let into the room
and asked to fetch the new item, he was very

00:08:34.099 --> 00:08:39.279
quickly able to infer which was the new toy
and fetched it straight away.

00:08:39.279 --> 00:08:43.610
Rico seemingly used a process of deduction
and elimination.

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:48.839
This is called “fast mapping”, a process
where one is able to quickly learn a new concept

00:08:48.839 --> 00:08:52.600
after a single exposure to brand new information.

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:56.330
Human toddlers do this all the time, it’s
how they learn.

00:08:56.330 --> 00:09:02.380
Even more amazingly Rico was able to fetch
the new toys again, four weeks later, having

00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:03.610
only seen them once.

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:10.060
Out of the six new items that Rico was shown,
four weeks prior, he remembered three of them,

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:12.560
four weeks later.

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:17.720
Interestingly three out of six is the same
rate at which adult humans are able to remember

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:20.660
things that they saw four weeks ago.

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:26.940
Chaser is another border collie who can reportedly
remember the name of 1,000 toys and can retrieve

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:29.380
each one of them, just like Rico.

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:32.060
But Chaser has another unique talent.

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:34.240
She is able to recognise verbs.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:39.610
From a young age Chaser’s owner, a retired
psychologist, trained Chaser to understand

00:09:39.610 --> 00:09:45.019
and utilise three verbs: nose, paw and fetch.

00:09:45.019 --> 00:09:50.010
When Chaser’s owner says “paw slinky”,
Chaser will go over to the toy named slinky

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:51.910
and put her paw on it.

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:57.810
Similarly, if “nose slinky” is said, Chaser
will put her nose on the slinky toy and when

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:01.560
“fetch slinky” is said, Chaser will fetch
the toy.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:07.790
Chaser’s owner is able to swap the verb
and the name of the toy for any one of 1,000

00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:12.850
different toys and Chaser will go over to
the correct toy and do the correct action

00:10:12.850 --> 00:10:15.860
almost one hundred percent of the time.

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:21.160
That’s about the same cognitive ability
as a three-year-old human child.

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:23.620
This also demonstrates something astonishing.

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:28.399
Chaser doesn’t simply remember each and
every command, it’s not just a cheap memory

00:10:28.399 --> 00:10:29.399
trick.

00:10:29.399 --> 00:10:34.620
Chaser’s brain is actually using cognitive
function to determine what to do in each given

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:35.720
situation.

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:38.600
This is no different to how a human brain
works.

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:41.730
Although this is rather basic stuff for an
adult human.

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:47.259
It’s an amazing display of cognitive ability
and logical inference for an animal.

00:10:47.259 --> 00:10:52.100
It demonstrates that dogs understand what
we say, provided they are given the opportunity

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:54.589
to learn these human-like concepts as a puppy.

00:10:54.589 --> 00:10:59.680
But that’s not different from a human, human’s
have to learn this stuff too, we aren’t

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:01.510
born knowing what “go get daddy a beer”
means.

00:11:01.510 --> 00:11:08.279
As a baby we learn the individual words that
construct that sentence and then as a toddler,

00:11:08.279 --> 00:11:13.279
we use our brain’s cognitive ability, especially
our fast mapping ability, to know what we

00:11:13.279 --> 00:11:18.290
should do when those words are arranged into
that sentence.

00:11:18.290 --> 00:11:20.880
Just like Chaser is doing.

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:25.740
Dog’s aren’t able to learn as fast or
to the same extent as humans, so realistically

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:29.589
their ability is capped compared to a human.

00:11:29.589 --> 00:11:33.889
However, provided they are given the correct
education and training from an early age,

00:11:33.889 --> 00:11:39.570
dogs most definitely can understand at least
a small percentage of what you say to them.

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:44.380
So when you say “time for ‘walkies’”
and your dog goes freaking mental.

00:11:44.380 --> 00:11:48.890
It may not just be because they have associated
the word “walkies” with running about

00:11:48.890 --> 00:11:54.050
outside with their beloved owner; there’s
actually some very basic level of understanding

00:11:54.050 --> 00:11:55.050
there.

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:59.759
But don’t think you can go and have full-blown,
esoteric conversations with your canine buddy.

00:11:59.759 --> 00:12:05.190
They may understand the odd word or two, but
first and foremost, dogs use smell, to communicate

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:07.940
and differentiate between objects and people.

00:12:07.940 --> 00:12:12.040
They’re going to understand a lot more of
what you’re trying to communicate to then,

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:16.380
if you roll around in the garden for ten minutes
then let them sniff you, than if you try to

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:19.540
explain to them why you had such a bad day
at work.

00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:27.170
But so far we’ve only talked about dogs,
horses and whales, what about other animals?

00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:31.380
After all the spectrum of animal cognition
spans the entire animal kingdom.

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:33.470
Take Koko the gorilla for example.

00:12:33.470 --> 00:12:39.220
Koko is a female gorilla who has learnt a
modified version of American Sign Language.

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:46.190
Koko was taught from an early age and now,
she can reportedly understand and use 1,000

00:12:46.190 --> 00:12:51.350
different signs of what her trainer calls
“Gorilla Sign Language” and she understands

00:12:51.350 --> 00:12:54.459
over 2,000 words of spoken English.

00:12:54.459 --> 00:12:59.170
Naturally Koko has been the subject of numerous
scientific studies, articles and books.

00:12:59.170 --> 00:13:04.290
But whether or not Koko actually understands
sign language in the same way a human does,

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:06.730
is a topic of hot debate.

00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:11.639
Some researchers argue that Koko hasn’t
actually mastered sign language at all and

00:13:11.639 --> 00:13:14.589
she doesn’t understand the words she is
signing.

00:13:14.589 --> 00:13:20.500
They insist that Koko’s human-like sign
language abilities are a result of operant

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:22.350
conditioning.

00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:26.069
Operant conditioning is when someone learns
to do something because there’s a reward

00:13:26.069 --> 00:13:27.069
at the end of it.

00:13:27.069 --> 00:13:32.000
For example, if you showed a toddler three
different coloured boxes, blue, green and

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:37.430
red and placed a sweet in the green box - the
toddler would then learn to always open the

00:13:37.430 --> 00:13:40.660
green box in the future, in order to get the
sweet.

00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:45.690
Koko may have simply learnt to make certain
shapes and signs with her hands because she

00:13:45.690 --> 00:13:48.230
is rewarded for doing so.

00:13:48.230 --> 00:13:53.160
Video evidence showed that Koko was also being
influenced by the Clever Hans effect.

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:58.569
Her trainers were giving her unconscious facial
clues to prompt her to make certain gestures

00:13:58.569 --> 00:14:00.670
with her hands.

00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:05.730
Despite all this, Koko’s trainers are adamant
that there’s more going on in Koko’s head

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:08.550
than researchers give her credit for.

00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:13.399
One piece of evidence which suggests a greater
level of cognition in Koko’s brain, occurred

00:14:13.399 --> 00:14:16.529
when Koko’s baby was taken away from her.

00:14:16.529 --> 00:14:23.019
The day after her baby was removed, she reportedly
signed the word “baby” to her keeper.

00:14:23.019 --> 00:14:27.750
This is known as displacement, the ability
to talk about objects that are not currently

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:32.379
present in the room and it’s something that
we thought was unique to humans and it’s

00:14:32.379 --> 00:14:34.959
very rarely observed in the animal kingdom.

00:14:34.959 --> 00:14:39.440
Also, Koko has been known to talk about new
objects that she hasn’t even been taught

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:41.240
how to sign.

00:14:41.240 --> 00:14:45.579
For example, Koko has never been taught the
sign language for the the word “ring”.

00:14:45.579 --> 00:14:53.090
But Koko combined the signs for “finger”
and “bracelet” to refer to a ring.

00:14:53.090 --> 00:14:58.709
If you think about it, a ring is just a tiny
bracelet for your finger, that’s pretty

00:14:58.709 --> 00:15:00.740
smart going Koko.

00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:05.180
Events such as this suggest that Koko has
a higher level of understanding of the words

00:15:05.180 --> 00:15:06.990
she is actually signing.

00:15:06.990 --> 00:15:11.690
But there’s a dark side that comes with
so much gorilla intelligence.

00:15:11.690 --> 00:15:18.860
Koko enjoys seeing human nipples and she often
asks her female caregivers using sign language,

00:15:18.860 --> 00:15:21.320
to show her their nipples.

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:27.639
This unusual behavior actually resulted in
a sexual harassment lawsuit by one of Koko’s

00:15:27.639 --> 00:15:30.050
female caregivers in 2005.

00:15:30.050 --> 00:15:35.750
Maybe it’s not such a good idea we try to
communicate with animals.

00:15:35.750 --> 00:15:40.509
Dolphins are often said to be one of the smartest
animals in the world and they certainly proved

00:15:40.509 --> 00:15:42.670
it in a 1984 study.

00:15:42.670 --> 00:15:45.930
Two bottlenose dolphins were taught human
language.

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:50.519
The first dolphin named Phoenix was taught
how to comprehend human speech.

00:15:50.519 --> 00:15:55.160
The second dolphin, Akeakamai, was taught
a form of sign language.

00:15:55.160 --> 00:16:00.220
Both dolphins were taught a large variety
of words, such as object names, actions and

00:16:00.220 --> 00:16:01.810
object modifiers.

00:16:01.810 --> 00:16:06.170
All of which could be combined and rearranged
into hundreds of unique sentences to form

00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:07.330
a command.

00:16:07.330 --> 00:16:12.399
For example, “swim to the blue ring” or
“pick up the red ring”.

00:16:12.399 --> 00:16:17.899
The commands were given to the dolphins using
computed generated voices and videos, to prevent

00:16:17.899 --> 00:16:19.710
the Clever Hans effect.

00:16:19.710 --> 00:16:24.660
Both dolphins were able to comprehend and
execute the given commands at a much higher

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:28.319
success rate than what would be considered
chance.

00:16:28.319 --> 00:16:31.600
Understanding words and simple one-word commands
is one thing.

00:16:31.600 --> 00:16:38.680
But for an animal to understand complex three-to-five
word commands and accurately follow them,

00:16:38.680 --> 00:16:42.690
is quite simply, astonishing.

00:16:42.690 --> 00:16:48.009
Experiments such as these prove that many
animals have an unprecedented level of understanding

00:16:48.009 --> 00:16:50.260
of human speech and communication.

00:16:50.260 --> 00:16:56.051
Up to now we’ve only explored a minute fraction
of intellect within the animal kingdom, who

00:16:56.051 --> 00:16:58.959
knows what some animals are really capable
of.

00:16:58.959 --> 00:17:02.430
A real life planet of the apes may be just
around the corner.

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:06.090
But until then you should probably watch what
you’re saying around your pets.

00:17:06.090 --> 00:17:10.150
They may be listening a bit more attentively
than you think.

